  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe problem is to find a step function h minimizing the maximum overlap integral C\u2085. Current methods like PSO may not efficiently explore the non-convex landscape. Instead, we reformulate the problem as a linear program (LP) to leverage convex optimization guarantees. The LP formulation linearizes the overlap objective by exploiting constraints and symmetry, allowing for efficient global optimization.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nLet h \u2208 \u211d^n, n points over [0,2], with constraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- sum(h) = n_points / 2\nMinimize C s.t. for all k:\nC \u2265 (1/n_points) * sum_{i} h[i] * (1 - h[(i + k) mod n])\nThe cross-correlation becomes a linear constraint by fixing k shifts in a circulant matrix. The LP is:\nminimize C\nsubject to:\nH * (I - H) \u2264 n_points * C\nh[i] \u2208 [0,1]\nsum(h) = n_points / 2\nwhere H is a binary overlap matrix for lag k.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na) Initialize n_points from parent or default to 100.\nb) Construct overlap matrices for all lags.\nc) Formulate LP with CVXPY using CLP or CBC solvers.\nd) Add constraints for each lag to bound C.\ne) Solve and verify feasibility, clipping to [0,1] if needed.\nf) Return h, C value, and n_points.\n\n4. Search choices\nUse n_points = len(initial_h_values) if available, else 100. Choose CLP solver for speed. Initialize with provided h if available. Set solver timeout to budget_s-60 for safety.\n\n5. Compute budget\nLP solve time dominates. Set solver timeout with 60s buffer. Compute progress every 60s, check for better solutions.\n\n6. Validation\nCheck h satisfies sum=1 and [0,1] constraints. Verify overlap constraints, recompute C to confirm.",
